{{Short description|none}}
The [[United States|U.S.]] {{nobreak|[[Florida|State of Florida]]}} currently has 35 [[Statistical area (United States)|statistical areas]] that have been delineated by the [[Office of Management and Budget]] (OMB). On July 21, 2023, the OMB delineated 7 [[combined statistical area]]s, 22 [[metropolitan statistical area]]s, and 6 [[micropolitan statistical area]]s in Florida.<ref name="OMB_20230721">{{cite web |subject=Revised Delineations of Metropolitan Statistical Areas, Micropolitan Statistical Areas, and Combined Statistical Areas, and Guidance on Uses of the Delineations of These Areas |date=July 21, 2023 |title=0MB BULLETIN NO. 23-01 |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/OMB-Bulletin-23-01.pdf |access-date=October 23, 2023 |publisher=[[Office of Management and Budget]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=OMB Bulletin |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/OMB-Bulletin-23-01.pdf}}</ref> As of 2023, the largest of these is the [[Miami-Port St. Lucie-Fort Lauderdale, FL CSA]], inclusive of the southeastern region of the state centered on [[Miami]].

